1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,028
Lesson 4.8.

2
00:00:02,408 --> 00:00:05,584
RBMs and contrastive divergence.

3
00:00:06,717 --> 00:00:10,285
The general Boltzmann machine
is hard to train: every step

4
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:13,977
requires equilibrium samples
from a fully connected network.

5
00:00:14,357 --> 00:00:18,877
In 2002, Hinton proposed two
simplifications that made it practical.

6
00:00:19,257 --> 00:00:22,339
Restrict the architecture, and
don't wait for equilibrium.

7
00:00:22,719 --> 00:00:27,586
The result, the restricted Boltzmann
machine trained with contrastive

8
00:00:27,586 --> 00:00:32,101
divergence, drove the revival of
deep learning in the mid-2000s.

9
00:00:33,217 --> 00:00:36,928
An RBM has a visible layer
and a hidden layer, with

10
00:00:36,928 --> 00:00:40,859
connections only between
layers, never within a layer.

11
00:00:41,239 --> 00:00:47,051
The energy is E = −vᵀWh − bᵀv − cᵀh.

12
00:00:47,431 --> 00:00:50,352
Since the graph is bipartite,
given the visible units the

13
00:00:50,352 --> 00:00:53,325
hidden ones are independent
of each other, and vice versa.

14
00:00:53,705 --> 00:01:08,032
Each conditional is a product of
sigmoids: p(h_j = 1 | v) = σ(c_j + vᵀW_j).

15
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:12,376
It is a two-colored graph:
block Gibbs is perfect here.

16
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:18,483
The exact rule is still data
correlations minus model correlations.

17
00:01:18,863 --> 00:01:22,254
The idea of contrastive
divergence is to approximate the

18
00:01:22,254 --> 00:01:25,766
model term by starting the
Gibbs chain at a data point and

19
00:01:25,766 --> 00:01:29,156
taking only k steps, instead
of waiting for equilibrium.

20
00:01:29,536 --> 00:01:34,452
With k equal to one: data, hidden,
reconstruction, hidden again.

21
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:38,991
The gradient is biased, but it has
low variance and is very cheap.

22
00:01:39,371 --> 00:01:42,546
In the persistent variant,
the chain is not restarted at

23
00:01:42,546 --> 00:01:45,381
the data: it keeps running
between learning steps.

24
00:01:46,483 --> 00:01:50,433
Let's train an RBM with nine
visible and twelve hidden units on

25
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:53,818
the fourteen three-by-three
bars-and-stripes patterns.

26
00:01:54,198 --> 00:01:57,855
With nine visible units we can
compute the exact log-likelihood.

27
00:01:58,235 --> 00:02:04,419
The optimum, spreading probability equally
over the fourteen patterns, is −2.64.

28
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:12,190
After six thousand steps, CD-1
reaches −3.15 and CD-10, −2.83.

29
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:16,070
More Gibbs steps reduce the
bias and give a better model.

30
00:02:17,183 --> 00:02:22,428
RBMs can be stacked: the hidden units of
one are the visible units of the next.

31
00:02:22,808 --> 00:02:26,920
Training layer by layer gives
a deep belief network, which

32
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:30,535
in 2006 showed that deep
networks could be trained.

33
00:02:30,915 --> 00:02:34,877
The wake–sleep algorithm and
Helmholtz machines are close

34
00:02:34,877 --> 00:02:38,838
relatives: a recognition
network that infers causes and a

35
00:02:38,838 --> 00:02:42,591
generative network that
dreams data, trained in turns.

36
00:02:43,717 --> 00:02:45,725
One CD-1 step with numbers.

37
00:02:46,105 --> 00:02:51,201
Suppose a weight with
data correlation ⟨v_i h_j⟩

38
00:02:51,201 --> 00:02:55,766
= 0.62 and, after one
reconstruction, 0.41.

39
00:02:56,146 --> 00:03:00,863
With learning rate 0.3, the
weight increases by 0.063.

40
00:03:01,243 --> 00:03:05,929
On pbit hardware, each block
Gibbs step is a single parallel

41
00:03:05,929 --> 00:03:10,694
update of one layer: one CD-1
step is four half clock phases.

42
00:03:11,817 --> 00:03:15,228
RBMs are ideal for pbit
hardware, because their

43
00:03:15,228 --> 00:03:18,277
bipartite graph is updated layer by layer.

44
00:03:18,657 --> 00:03:21,501
They have been used to learn features, in

45
00:03:21,501 --> 00:03:25,109
recommender systems and to
represent quantum states.

46
00:03:25,489 --> 00:03:28,116
In lab six you will compare CD and its

47
00:03:28,116 --> 00:03:31,574
persistent version using
the exact log-likelihood.

48
00:03:32,683 --> 00:03:36,400
Common mistakes: believing
CD follows the true gradient.

49
00:03:36,780 --> 00:03:41,765
Using large rates with persistent CD,
because the chains cannot keep up with

50
00:03:41,765 --> 00:03:46,028
the model and training diverges;
we saw it in our own experiment.

51
00:03:46,408 --> 00:03:50,730
Sampling the hidden units in the positive
phase instead of using probabilities.

52
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:54,765
And monitoring the reconstruction
error instead of the likelihood.

53
00:03:55,883 --> 00:04:00,105
In summary: the RBM restricts
the connections to a bipartite

54
00:04:00,105 --> 00:04:03,763
graph, and its conditionals
factorize into sigmoids.

55
00:04:04,143 --> 00:04:08,378
Contrastive divergence
approximates the model phase with

56
00:04:08,378 --> 00:04:11,932
k Gibbs steps from the
data: biased, but cheap.

57
00:04:12,312 --> 00:04:16,187
Stacking RBMs gave the first
trainable deep networks.

58
00:04:17,317 --> 00:04:20,573
To practice: derive
the hidden conditional.

59
00:04:20,953 --> 00:04:23,172
Write the free energy
of the visible units.

60
00:04:23,552 --> 00:04:25,130
Explain the bias of CD.

61
00:04:25,510 --> 00:04:29,848
And count the states needed for the
partition function with nine visible units.
